<!--
  THIS FILE IS A TEMPLATE, NOT THE DEPLOYED CONFIG.

  |USERPROFILE| is substituted with the interactive user's profile directory by
  scripts/install-deletion-forensics.ps1, which writes the rendered result to
  C:\ProgramData\Sysmon\filedelete-forensics.xml. Do not deploy this file directly.

  It has to be a template because Sysmon cannot do it. The condition set is a fixed list -
  is, is not, contains, begin with, end with, image, and a few more - with no environment-
  variable expansion, no wildcard and no per-user construct anywhere in the schema (checked
  against `Sysmon64.exe -s`, schema 4.91). Two of the three include rules could be written
  machine-agnostically as `contains \AppData\Local\` and `contains \Documents\`, but the
  third - a dot-directory immediately under a profile root - cannot be expressed in that
  grammar at all, so substitution is required rather than merely convenient.

  Until 2026-09-10 this file hardcoded one profile name in 24 rules. On any machine whose
  user is not named Admin the include list matched nothing, so the sensor recorded ZERO
  deletions while -Verify reported fully green and the smoke test passed. A forensics sensor
  that is verifiably, invisibly blind is the worst failure this tooling can have, and the
  repo ships a public bootstrap that recommends installing it on a fresh workstation.

  The pipe character is deliberate: it is illegal in every Windows path, so "no placeholder
  survived rendering" is a total, machine-checkable assertion rather than a hopeful one.
-->
<Sysmon schemaversion="4.90">
  <!--
    Deletion forensics for this workstation, written 2026-09-09 after a mass profile deletion
    (16:00-17:37) could not be attributed: Sysmon was not installed, File System auditing was
    off, and the USN journal retained under two hours. This config exists so a repeat is
    answerable.

    EVENT 26 (FileDeleteDetected), NOT EVENT 23 (FileDelete). This is the single most important
    line in the file. Event 23 ARCHIVES a copy of every deleted file into an archive directory
    before logging it, which on a machine that deletes build output, node_modules and package
    caches all day would consume the disk it is meant to protect. Event 26 records the same
    who/what/when and copies nothing.

    Sysmon fires this per FILE, not per directory, so a recursive tree delete appears as a burst
    of events sharing one Image and ProcessGuid. That is the signature to look for, and it is
    more useful than a single directory-removal record would be.

    Scope is the user profile, because that is what was lost. The exclusions below are churn that
    would otherwise drown the signal - a log nobody can read is a log nobody reads.

    RETENTION TARGET: 48 HOURS of deletion history. Two days is the useful window for "what
    happened to my files"; 72 was the first guess and was never grounded in anything. Every
    exclusion below was MEASURED before it was added, and the weekly dashboard reports actual
    retention from the log's own span and size - because two hand estimates were already wrong
    by an order of magnitude.
  -->
  <EventFiltering>

    <!--
      ProcessCreate, deliberately an INCLUDE list rather than an exclude list.

      Measured before this change: 997 events/minute, of which only 42 were deletions. The other
      955 were process create/terminate on a busy dev box - about 8 process launches a second
      during a build. At that rate a 1 GB log holds ~12 hours, against a 48-hour target.

      Event 26 already names the deleting Image, so ProcessCreate is not load-bearing for "who";
      it adds the COMMAND LINE and the parent, which is what turns "powershell.exe" into the
      actual command. That is only worth its volume for the handful of programs capable of
      running a mass delete: shells, script hosts, installers, bulk copy tools, and anything
      executing out of Temp or Downloads.
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="proc-create" groupRelation="or">
      <ProcessCreate onmatch="include">
        <Image condition="end with">\powershell.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\pwsh.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\cmd.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\wscript.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\cscript.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\mshta.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\rundll32.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\regsvr32.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\msiexec.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\cleanmgr.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\robocopy.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\xcopy.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\bash.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\wsl.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\python.exe</Image>
        <!-- Anything running out of a scratch or download location. -->
        <Image condition="contains">\AppData\Local\Temp\</Image>
        <Image condition="contains">\Downloads\</Image>
        <!--
          The Unix coreutils that actually DELETE. These were missing, and their absence was a
          real hole: a live test deleting a file from the agent shell recorded the deletion
          against C:\Anthropic\.Git\usr\bin\rm.exe - bash forks rm as its own process, so bash
          is never the deleting image. Event 26 therefore named rm.exe while nothing anywhere
          recorded WHAT rm was asked to remove.
          Their own command line is better evidence than the bash wrapper's, because it is
          already resolved: "rm -rf /c/Users/Admin/.ollama" rather than a shell string.
          Cost measured before adding: ZERO occurrences in 40,000 sampled ProcessCreate events.
          git.exe is here for `git clean -fdx`, which removes more than people expect.
        -->
        <Image condition="end with">\rm.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\rmdir.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\unlink.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\shred.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\mv.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\find.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\xargs.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\git.exe</Image>
      </ProcessCreate>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
      Agent shell churn, excluded. Measured over 20,688 ProcessCreate events:

        C:\Anthropic\.Git\usr\bin\bash.exe   11,869   57.4%
        C:\Windows\System32\cmd.exe           5,567   26.9%   (spawned by the above)
        C:\Anthropic\.Git\bin\bash.exe        1,542    7.5%
        ...git.exe                              474    2.2%

      That is 94% of all process telemetry generated by a coding agent running shell commands,
      and it was crowding out the thing this log exists for: ProcessCreate was 73.5% of the
      whole log and projected retention had fallen to 16 hours against a 48-hour target.
      Excluding it took the measured rate to 4,620 events/hour, projecting ~124 hours at 2 GB.

      The ParentImage rule is what makes this work - cmd.exe is a real mass-deletion vector and
      is NOT excluded on its own, only when the agent's bash spawned it.

      What this costs, stated plainly: the bash wrapper's command line. The deletion itself is
      untouched - event 26 still records every path, the pid and the user - and since the
      coreutils above are now included, `rm -rf /c/Users/Admin/.ollama` is captured directly,
      which is better evidence than the shell string that produced it. Excluding the shells
      moved projected retention from 16 hours to ~124.

      NARROWED so it cannot swallow the coreutils above. An earlier version excluded everything
      under C:\Anthropic\.Git\ by prefix, and by ParentImage too - which would have discarded
      rm.exe both ways, since bash is always its parent. Exclude wins over include in Sysmon, so
      that broad rule would have silently cancelled the most valuable evidence in the file.
      Only the SHELLS are dropped now, plus cmd.exe when the agent's bash spawned it; cmd.exe
      on its own remains watched, because it is a real mass-deletion vector.
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="proc-create-noise" groupRelation="or">
      <ProcessCreate onmatch="exclude">
        <Image condition="end with">\.Git\usr\bin\bash.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="end with">\.Git\bin\bash.exe</Image>
        <Rule groupRelation="and">
          <Image condition="end with">\cmd.exe</Image>
          <ParentImage condition="begin with">C:\Anthropic\.Git\</ParentImage>
        </Rule>
      </ProcessCreate>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
      ProcessTerminate off entirely: an include rule with no children matches nothing. It was
      roughly half the volume and answers no question that ProcessCreate plus Event 26 does not.
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="proc-term" groupRelation="or">
      <ProcessTerminate onmatch="include" />
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
      Scope. A mass deletion is defined by BREADTH - the 2026-09-09 event hit 16 profile dotdirs
      plus 11 directories under AppData\Local. So complete coverage is not required to detect one;
      reliable coverage of QUIET, valuable paths is, because there any deletion at all is
      anomalous. Noisy caches make the worst sentinels: deletions in them are normal, so they
      cost log space and raise the threshold at which a real burst becomes visible.

      Kept broad rather than enumerated per directory, deliberately. "Anything beginning
      |USERPROFILE|\." covers a tool installed next week without anyone remembering to add it;
      a hand-maintained list of 20 dotdirs would have been missing .antigravity and .u2net until
      the day they mattered.

      Dropped from an earlier version: AppData\Roaming (broad app churn, and nothing was lost
      there on 09-09 - the PSReadline history that survived lives in it) and all of ProgramData
      (service churn; Backblaze alone was 1062 of a 1500-event sample).
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="delete-include" groupRelation="or">
      <FileDeleteDetected onmatch="include">
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\.</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\AppData\Local\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\Documents\</TargetFilename>
      </FileDeleteDetected>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!-- Churn. Excluded to keep 48 hours legible, not because it is uninteresting.
         Note the trade: a mass deletion still lands in the INCLUDE set above via .ollama,
         .cargo, .nuget, DevToolbox and the rest, so the burst signature survives these. -->
    <RuleGroup name="delete-exclude" groupRelation="or">
      <FileDeleteDetected onmatch="exclude">
        <!-- The two loudest by a wide margin. -->
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\AppData\Local\Temp\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\AppData\Local\Packages\</TargetFilename>
        <!-- Browser and shell caches: continuous, never the thing we are chasing. -->
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\AppData\Local\Microsoft\Windows\INetCache\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\AppData\Local\Microsoft\Windows\Explorer\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\AppData\Local\Microsoft\Windows\WebCache\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\AppData\Local\Microsoft\Edge\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\AppData\Local\Google\Chrome\</TargetFilename>
        <!-- GPU shader caches rewrite constantly. -->
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\AppData\Local\D3DSCache\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\AppData\Local\NVIDIA\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\AppData\Local\AMD\</TargetFilename>
        <!-- Editor and agent working state, rewritten many times a minute. -->
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\AppData\Roaming\Code\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\.vscode-server\data\</TargetFilename>
        <!-- Build and dependency churn anywhere in the tree. -->
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\node_modules\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\.git\objects\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\__pycache__\</TargetFilename>
        <!--
          Package-manager caches. These ARE valuable and .nuget/.cache were both damaged on
          09-09, so excluding them is a deliberate trade, not an oversight: they rewrite
          constantly during any build, and a mass deletion still trips .ollama, .gemini,
          .agents, .ssh, DevToolbox and a dozen other quiet paths in the same burst. We lose
          nothing in detection and get the log back.
          Scoped to the churning SUBdirectory in each case, so deleting the parent still shows.
        -->
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\.nuget\packages\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\.npm\_cacache\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\.cargo\registry\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\.cache\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\.bun\install\cache\</TargetFilename>
        <!--
          Five C:\ProgramData\ exclusions used to live here - Defender, Sysmon, Backblaze,
          Whesvc, Rainmeter-GPU. They were REMOVED, not because that churn stopped mattering
          but because ProgramData was dropped from the include list above, which made every one
          of them unreachable. A rule no input can ever match is worse than no rule: it reads
          like coverage in review and nobody checks it again. (pc-maintenance hit exactly this
          with a long-path pattern the UNC rule already caught; its BACKLOG item 2 has the
          post-mortem.) Backblaze is confirmed silent since the include change: 1,566 events
          in the historical log, 0 in the twenty minutes after it.

          .NET SDK telemetry - 160 of a 1500-event sample, the largest single source at the
          time. Note this excludes ONE SUBDIRECTORY, not .dotnet itself: .dotnet was a victim
          on 2026-09-09 and must stay watched.
        -->
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\.dotnet\TelemetryStorageService\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\AppData\Local\Dropbox\</TargetFilename>
        <!--
          Vendor apps churning their own Chromium caches. Measured over 5,269 deletions:
          Razer 720, Steam 380, Backblaze 1,566 - together 51% of the entire log.

          Excluded by PATH, not by Image, and the measurement is what justifies that: all three
          had ZERO deletions outside their own directories. A path rule therefore removes the
          noise while leaving these processes fully visible if they ever touch anything else,
          which an Image rule would not. Worth keeping that distinction - RazerAppEngine.exe
          deleting its service-worker cache is noise; RazerAppEngine.exe deleting .ssh is the
          exact event this whole thing exists to catch.
        -->
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\AppData\Local\Razer\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="begin with">|USERPROFILE|\AppData\Local\Steam\</TargetFilename>
      </FileDeleteDetected>
    </RuleGroup>

  </EventFiltering>
</Sysmon>
